    "text": "In the Celtic view the Otherworld was the realm where gods and fairies lived. This idea was later joined with the Christian land of the dead. The dead and the fairies grew hard to tell apart. Both could step into our world to work mischief. People often set the land of the dead far off in the western sea, at times as an unstable island. Others imagined the dead at rest in their graves, ready to rise when the season or need came. Some read in the tradition a belief in rebirth, which explains the small concern over where the dead lay. Warriors felt no fear because death was a passage to another life. Before taking a new body the dead could return. Folktales told of young mothers coming back each night to nurse infants, and of lovers returning to watch over the beloved. Most often the dead came back on Samhain, the first of November. Then the veils between the worlds hung thin. A murdered person could return to demand vengeance, or a family member to bestow a boon.",
